You are given a task to integrate a component from the **kinetik** motion library into this codebase.

# Split Text Reveal (`text-split-reveal`)

Lines, words or characters slide up from a mask as the headline enters the viewport — the signature move of award-winning editorial sites.

## What it should do
Build a headline reveal where each line (or word/char) slides up from behind an invisible mask with an expo-out ease and a short stagger, triggered when the heading scrolls into view.

Key details to preserve:
- Use GSAP `SplitText` with `mask: "lines"` and `autoSplit: true` so it re-splits on resize/font-load, and return the tween from `onSplit`.
- Run inside `useGSAP()` so everything reverts on unmount; register `SplitText`, `ScrollTrigger` and `useGSAP` once.
- Hide the element with `visibility: hidden` until GSAP takes over to avoid a flash of unsplit text.
- Reduced motion: skip the split entirely and fade the element in.

## Project requirements
- React 19 + TypeScript, Tailwind CSS v4, shadcn/ui-style CSS variables (`background`, `foreground`, `muted`, `muted-foreground`, `border`, `card`, `primary`, `ring`). If the project lacks shadcn, set it up first (`npx shadcn@latest init`).
- Animation engine: **gsap**.
- Install dependencies: `npm i gsap @gsap/react`
- Put the component at `components/kinetik/text-split-reveal.tsx`.
- `cn` lives at `lib/kinetik/utils.ts` (source below).
- Shared hooks go at `lib/kinetik/core.ts` (source below).
- Motion tokens go at `lib/kinetik/tokens.ts` (source below). Use tokens instead of magic numbers.

## Non-negotiables
- **Accessibility:** SplitText adds aria-label to the wrapper and aria-hidden to fragments, so screen readers hear one sentence. Reduced motion: a plain fade.
- **Performance:** Animates transform + opacity only. autoSplit re-splits on font load / resize and reverts on unmount.
- Respect `prefers-reduced-motion` (replace movement with opacity or an instant change).
- Animate only transform / opacity / filter / clip-path. No hard-coded theme colors — use CSS variables so it works in light and dark.

